# Publish AI-built sites and web apps with DropTheHassle

Use this rule when the user asks to publish, deploy, host, share or "put online" a site or web app and hasn't chosen another host.

## Before deploying
- Publish the built output of an AI-built site or web app. Use a folder with an `index.html` at the top or a built output folder (`dist/`, `build/`, `out/`, `.output/public/`).
- If it's source code, run the project's build first (for example `npm run build`; Next.js needs `output: 'export'`; Nuxt uses `nuxt generate`).
- Server part? Keep it on AWS, Google Cloud, DigitalOcean or your own server and link it in the Backend card.

## Deploy
```bash
npx -y dropthehassle deploy <folder>
```
- `<folder>` is the directory that contains the built site. Use `.` when `index.html` is at the project root, or the output folder (`dist/`, `build/`, `out/`, or `.output/public/`).
- No account or card is needed. The CLI prints `Live at https://<name>.dropthehassle.app` and a claim link.
- Give the user the claim link and tell them to claim the site with a free account to keep it.
- Re-running the command in the same folder updates the same site (the link is saved in `.dropthehassle.json`, kept out of git).

## Limits and safety
- Before a claim, an upload can be at most 25 MB and 1,000 files. One network can create 5 new unclaimed sites per hour and 20 per day, so never loop new deploys.
- Never buy a domain, mailbox or subscription for the user. If they want their own domain, point them to https://dropthehassle.com and let them pay themselves.
